## [ISSUE] getBaseConditions returns unparenthesized OR fragments, so a predicate the caller appends only applies to one branch

**Severity:** MEDIUM. A caller that adds any predicate (date range, status) to the base conditions gets wrong results: team bookings outside the requested range are counted. Results stay inside the authorization scope, because `A OR (B AND x)` is still a subset of `A OR B`.

### The Problem

```ts
if (authConditions && filterConditions) {
  return Prisma.sql`(${authConditions}) AND (${filterConditions})`;
} else if (authConditions) {
  return authConditions;          // <- returned bare
} else if (filterConditions) {    // unreachable: auth is always a truthy Prisma.Sql
  return filterConditions;
}
```

`buildOrgAuthorizationCondition` and `buildTeamAuthorizationCondition` both reduce into `(${acc}) OR (${condition})` with no outer parentheses. The combined branch at L73 wraps both sides, but the auth-only branch at L75 returns the OR bare. (The filter-only branch at L76-77 can never run, because `buildAuthorizationConditions` always returns at least `NOTHING_CONDITION`.)

### Theory / Proof

In PostgreSQL, `AND` binds tighter than `OR` (see the PostgreSQL docs, "Operator Precedence"). The method is called *base* conditions, so callers are expected to compose it:

```sql
WHERE ("teamId" = ANY($1)) AND ("isTeamBooking" = true) OR ("userId" = ANY($2)) AND ("isTeamBooking" = false) AND "createdAt" >= $3
```

This parses as `(team branch) OR (user branch AND createdAt >= $3)`. The date bound is dropped for every team booking in the org.

### What happens today vs. what will break

No production caller exists yet. `findMany` was removed and only the integration test uses `getBaseConditions`, with `WHERE ${baseConditions}` and nothing appended. That test also uses user scope plus a filter, so it goes through the wrapped L73 branch. The first insights query that does `WHERE ${base} AND "createdAt" BETWEEN ...` for org or team scope without filters will return wrong counts.

### Research

Libraries that compose SQL, such as Knex's `where(fn)` grouping and the Prisma `AND: []` object API this PR replaces, always group sub-expressions. The previous object-based `findMany` put auth inside `AND: [auth, filter]`, which Prisma renders with parentheses. This PR loses that guarantee.

### Recommendation

Always parenthesize what gets returned (the unreachable filter-only branch can be removed):

```ts
if (authConditions && filterConditions) {
  return Prisma.sql`((${authConditions}) AND (${filterConditions}))`;
}
return Prisma.sql`(${authConditions})`;
```

Also add a test that runs `WHERE ${base} AND "createdAt" < ...` for org scope with no filters.

## [GAP ANALYSIS] Constructor option type was loosened from a discriminated union to a flat type, so a missing teamId no longer fails type checking

**Severity:** LOW. A misconfigured caller gets an empty insights dashboard at runtime where it used to get a compile error.

### The Problem

```ts
export type InsightsBookingServicePublicOptions = {
  scope: "user" | "org" | "team";
  userId: number;
  orgId: number;
  teamId?: number;
};
```

The constructor used to take `InsightsBookingServiceOptions`, which is `z.infer` of the discriminated union. That type requires `teamId` when `scope === "team"`. Now `new InsightsBookingService({ options: { scope: "team", userId, orgId } })` compiles. `safeParse` fails (L62-63), `this.options` becomes `null`, and `buildAuthorizationConditions` returns `NOTHING_CONDITION`. Nothing is logged or thrown.

### Theory / Proof

TypeScript discriminated unions exist to catch this kind of mismatch at compile time. A flat type with an optional field has no discriminant, so it can't express "required when scope is team".

### What happens today vs. what will break

The PR has no callers outside tests, so nothing breaks yet. A future tRPC handler that forgets `teamId` would ship and show zero bookings with no error.

### Research

`InsightsRoutingService` (packages/lib/server/service/insightsRouting.ts) still types its constructor with the zod-inferred union. That makes this service inconsistent with its sibling.

### Recommendation

If callers only need a looser *input* type, derive it from the schema instead of hand-writing it:

```ts
export type InsightsBookingServicePublicOptions = z.input<typeof insightsBookingServiceOptionsSchema>;
```

Otherwise, keep `options: InsightsBookingServiceOptions` in the constructor signature.
